"series-ee-bond-calculator":{title:"Series EE Bond Calculator",sections:[
{heading:"What an EE bond actually earns",text:["Series EE savings bonds are nonmarketable U.S. Treasury securities. Their value does not move with a quoted bond price, and you cannot sell them on an exchange. A calculator should therefore model the issue amount, the Treasury-assigned fixed rate for the bond, the holding period, and the special Treasury guarantee that eligible electronic EE bonds held for 20 years will be worth at least twice their issue price. That guarantee can dominate the ordinary fixed-rate accrual when the stated rate is low."]},
{heading:"Fixed-rate accrual versus the 20-year guarantee",text:["The ordinary path compounds the bond\u2019s fixed rate over time, but the 20-year rule creates a discontinuity. Immediately before the guarantee date, accrued value can be materially below twice the purchase amount; at the 20-year point Treasury can make a one-time adjustment so the value reaches the guaranteed level. A projection that merely compounds the stated rate can therefore understate a qualifying bond\u2019s value at year 20. After that adjustment, interest continues under the rules applicable to the bond until final maturity."]},
{heading:"Issue date is a core input",text:["EE bond rules vary by issue period, so the issue date is not cosmetic. Older paper bonds can follow different rate-setting and denomination conventions from newer electronic bonds. The calculator\u2019s assumptions should match the bond actually owned rather than applying today\u2019s purchase rules to every historical bond. For an existing bond, the most reliable starting data are the issue date, issue price or denomination, and current value shown in Treasury records; for a hypothetical new purchase, use the current Treasury terms."]},
{heading:"Early redemption changes the return",text:["EE bonds cannot be redeemed during the first 12 months. Redeeming before five years generally causes a penalty equal to the most recent three months of interest. That means a short holding-period estimate needs more than compound interest: it needs the lockup and penalty rules. A user comparing an EE bond with a savings account or CD should compare the amount actually available on the intended redemption date, not the value that would exist if the bond were held without penalty."]},
{heading:"Why the implied 20-year return matters",text:["Doubling over exactly 20 years corresponds to an annualized compound return of roughly 3.53%, because 2^(1/20)-1 is about 0.0353. This is useful as a reasonableness check when the 20-year guarantee applies. It does not mean the bond credits 3.53% every year; the account may grow at its stated fixed rate and receive an adjustment at the guarantee date. The distinction matters for anyone who may redeem before year 20, because the implied guaranteed annualized return is realized only by satisfying the holding requirement."]},
{heading:"Federal tax treatment and deferral",text:["EE bond interest is generally subject to federal income tax but exempt from state and local income taxes. Many individual owners defer reporting the interest until redemption, final maturity, or another taxable disposition, although annual reporting can be elected under tax rules. A calculator showing pretax growth should not label that amount as spendable after-tax wealth. The relevant after-tax value depends on the owner\u2019s federal marginal rate in the year interest is recognized and on whether any special exclusion is available."]},
{heading:"Education tax exclusion is conditional",text:["Interest on qualifying Series EE bonds may be excludable from federal income when statutory education-savings-bond requirements are met, including ownership, age-at-issue, qualified higher-education expenses, filing status, and income phaseouts. This is not an automatic education benefit attached to every EE bond. If the calculator does not collect those facts, it should present the exclusion as a possible tax consideration rather than subtracting tax by default. Users planning around the exclusion should verify the applicable tax-year limits and eligible expenses."]},
{heading:"Compare EE bonds with the correct alternatives",text:["An EE bond is best compared with alternatives over the same horizon and on the same tax basis. A 20-year EE strategy can look different from a five-year CD ladder, Treasury note, I bond, money-market fund, or high-yield savings account because liquidity, rate resets, inflation exposure, and taxes differ. The EE guarantee rewards patience but sacrifices access. A fair comparison therefore asks not only which ending balance is highest, but whether the investor can realistically leave the money untouched long enough to receive the guarantee."]},
{heading:"Inflation can erode purchasing power",text:["EE bonds promise nominal dollars, not a purchasing-power target. If inflation averages 3% while the investor realizes an annualized return only modestly above that level, the real increase in wealth can be small. Conversely, low inflation makes the same nominal guarantee more valuable in real terms. For long-range goals such as college or a future home purchase, users should compare the projected bond value with an inflation-adjusted future cost rather than assuming that doubling the account means doubling what the money can buy."]},
{heading:"Worked example of the guarantee",text:["Suppose $10,000 of eligible electronic EE bonds are purchased and the fixed-rate accrual alone would produce only $12,500 by year 20. If the Treasury guarantee applies, the value at the 20-year anniversary would be adjusted to at least $20,000. If the same bonds were redeemed at year 10, however, the owner would receive the redemption value determined under the bond rules at that time, not half of the guaranteed $20,000. This example shows why holding period is the decisive variable."]},
{heading:"Final maturity and reinvestment risk",text:["EE bonds earn interest only for a finite life, commonly up to 30 years for modern issues. Once a bond reaches final maturity, leaving it unredeemed does not create an endless compounding stream. Long-term plans should therefore include what happens after maturity: spending the proceeds, rolling them into another Treasury security, or investing elsewhere. The return on that next investment is unknown today, which creates reinvestment risk that a single-bond projection cannot eliminate."]},
{heading:"Common EE bond calculation mistakes",text:["Frequent mistakes include treating the face amount of an old paper bond as the original cash invested, assuming the 20-year doubling guarantee applies proportionally at every earlier year, forgetting the first-year redemption restriction, ignoring the under-five-year interest penalty, and comparing a tax-deferred EE value with an after-tax bank yield. Another error is applying current issue terms to an older bond. Each of these can produce a plausible number that does not match Treasury redemption value."]},
{heading:"How to use the result for a decision",text:["Use the calculator to answer a dated question: what could this specific bond or purchase be worth if redeemed in year 5, year 10, year 20, or at final maturity? Then compare those dates with the date the cash is actually needed. The 20-year result deserves special attention because of the guarantee, while earlier dates emphasize liquidity and credited interest. For an existing bond, Treasury\u2019s official value should be the authoritative check; the calculator is most useful for planning scenarios around that verified starting point."]},
{heading:"EE bonds versus I bonds",text:["EE bonds and Series I savings bonds solve different inflation problems. EE bonds issued under current structures use a fixed rate plus the potential 20-year doubling guarantee, while I bonds use a composite rate tied partly to inflation and do not promise the same 20-year doubling adjustment. An investor expecting to hold exactly 20 years may value the EE guarantee; an investor primarily concerned with preserving purchasing power may analyze I bonds differently. Purchase limits, redemption restrictions, tax treatment, and issue terms should all be compared for the intended holding period."]},
{heading:"Gift bonds and ownership details",text:["Savings bonds can be purchased as gifts through Treasury systems, but registration and ownership determine who owns the security and who may have tax obligations. A projection should be tied to the actual owner and issue date rather than to the person who supplied the purchase money. Beneficiary and co-owner designations also affect estate handling but do not change the mathematical accrual rate. For planning, keep valuation questions separate from legal ownership questions and verify registration directly in Treasury records."]},
{heading:"Why a smooth chart can be misleading",text:["A chart that draws one smooth compound-growth curve from purchase through year 30 can conceal the special year-20 adjustment. For low fixed-rate bonds, the economically important shape may include slow accrual followed by a guarantee adjustment. If the site displays a chart, marking the 12-month lockup, five-year penalty boundary, 20-year guarantee point, and final maturity gives users much more information than a generic exponential curve. These dates correspond to real decisions about access and return."]},
