"fire-number-calculator":{title:"FIRE Number Calculator",sections:[
{heading:"The FIRE number is a spending-based target",text:["Financial independence calculations begin with annual spending, not salary. The core question is how large an invested portfolio may be needed to support the portion of future spending that must come from investments. If expected annual retirement spending is $60,000 and no other income is counted, a simple 4% rule produces $1.5 million because $60,000 / 0.04 = $1,500,000. Changing the withdrawal assumption to 3.5% raises the target to about $1.714 million, showing why the chosen rate matters."]},
{heading:"Separate current spending from retirement spending",text:["Current expenses are a starting point, but retirement can remove commuting, payroll taxes, or mortgage payments while adding health insurance, travel, hobbies, and other costs. A useful FIRE estimate builds a retirement budget category by category rather than multiplying current spending blindly. Housing, food, transportation, insurance, taxes, healthcare, dependents, and discretionary spending deserve explicit assumptions. The more accurately future spending is estimated, the more meaningful the portfolio target becomes."]},
{heading:"Withdrawal rate is not a guaranteed yield",text:["A 4% withdrawal rule is a planning convention derived from historical portfolio research, not an interest rate promised by the market. It normally refers to an initial withdrawal percentage with later withdrawals adjusted under a specified policy. Outcomes depend on asset allocation, fees, taxes, inflation, retirement length, and market sequence. Someone retiring at 40 may need a different margin of safety from someone retiring at 65 because the portfolio may need to support several additional decades."]},
{heading:"Sequence-of-returns risk matters after retirement",text:["Two retirees can earn the same long-run average return and experience very different outcomes if losses occur at different times. Large declines early in retirement are especially damaging because withdrawals remove shares while prices are depressed, leaving fewer assets to participate in recovery. This is sequence risk. A FIRE number based only on average return does not capture it. Conservative withdrawal assumptions, flexible spending, cash reserves, part-time income, or dynamic withdrawal rules can improve resilience when early markets are poor."]},
{heading:"Inflation affects both the goal and the journey",text:["If the calculator expresses the FIRE number in today\u2019s dollars, future spending should be interpreted in real purchasing-power terms. If it projects nominal dollars, both spending and portfolio growth need consistent inflation assumptions. Mixing a nominal investment return with uninflated future expenses makes the target look too easy. For example, $60,000 of annual spending today would be roughly $80,600 after ten years at 3% inflation. Long accumulation periods make this distinction especially important."]},
{heading:"Other reliable income can reduce portfolio withdrawals",text:["Social Security, pensions, annuity income, rental cash flow, or other dependable sources may cover part of retirement spending. If $60,000 of spending is expected but a pension reliably supplies $20,000, the portfolio may need to fund only the remaining $40,000, subject to timing and taxes. Income that begins years after early retirement should not be subtracted from every year immediately. Bridge years before benefits start often require a larger accessible portfolio or a separate cash-flow plan."]},
{heading:"Taxes belong inside the spending estimate",text:["Retirement spending is usually stated as cash the household needs to live on, but withdrawals from traditional retirement accounts can create taxable income. Roth withdrawals may have different tax treatment, and taxable brokerage sales can generate capital gains. Therefore a $70,000 lifestyle may require more than $70,000 of gross withdrawals depending on account mix and tax circumstances. A simple FIRE calculator may not model taxes directly, so users should include an estimated tax budget or run a separate tax projection."]},
{heading:"Account accessibility matters for early retirees",text:["A person can be a millionaire on paper yet have limited penalty-free access to retirement funds before standard retirement ages. FIRE planning therefore considers where assets are held: taxable brokerage accounts, Roth contribution basis, employer plans, HSAs, and traditional IRAs can have different access rules. Strategies such as substantially equal periodic payments or Roth conversion ladders involve detailed tax rules and timing. The FIRE number measures wealth needed; it does not by itself solve the cash-access problem during early retirement."]},
{heading:"Savings rate determines time to FIRE",text:["During accumulation, the gap between after-tax income and spending drives how much can be invested. A higher savings rate helps twice: more money is invested each year and the lifestyle that portfolio must eventually support may be lower. Return assumptions matter, but savings behavior can dominate in the early years. When using a time-to-FIRE estimate, test a lower investment return and a higher expense path rather than relying on one optimistic projection."]},
{heading:"Worked FIRE target example",text:["Assume a household expects $72,000 of annual retirement spending, including taxes and healthcare. It expects $12,000 of dependable annual net rental income, leaving $60,000 for the portfolio. At a 4% initial withdrawal assumption, the simple target is $1.5 million. At 3.5%, it becomes about $1.714 million. If the household also wants a separate $100,000 reserve for major home repairs and near-term cash, that reserve should be added rather than pretending the same dollars simultaneously fund recurring withdrawals."]},
{heading:"Healthcare deserves its own scenario",text:["People leaving employer coverage before Medicare eligibility can face significant insurance premiums, deductibles, and out-of-pocket costs. Marketplace subsidies can depend on household income and policy rules, which makes taxable-income planning important. Healthcare also tends to rise differently from general consumer prices. A FIRE plan should therefore test at least one scenario with higher medical costs. Underestimating healthcare can reduce the apparent spending need precisely during the years when early retirees have fewer employer benefits."]},
{heading:"Housing assumptions can move the target dramatically",text:["A paid-off home can reduce recurring housing costs, but property taxes, insurance, maintenance, utilities, and major repairs remain. Renters face rent inflation and relocation risk. Someone planning to move to a lower-cost region should not count the savings until the move is realistically budgeted. Because housing is often the largest expense category, a $1,000 monthly difference changes annual spending by $12,000 and changes a 4%-rule FIRE target by roughly $300,000."]},
{heading:"Build margin for irregular expenses",text:["Annual budgets often omit cars, roofs, appliances, family support, legal costs, and other expenses that occur infrequently but predictably over decades. Converting those items into annual sinking-fund amounts produces a more honest spending estimate. A target based only on routine monthly bills can be deceptively low. The portfolio needs to support the entire lifestyle over time, not just an unusually cheap year immediately before retirement."]},
{heading:"Interpret FIRE as a range, not a finish line",text:["A calculated target is best viewed as a planning range tied to assumptions. Show what the number becomes at several withdrawal rates and spending levels, then identify which assumptions the household can control. Recalculate after major changes in housing, family size, healthcare, income, or market value. Reaching a headline number is not sufficient if the underlying budget is stale or the portfolio is concentrated. A robust FIRE plan combines a realistic spending model, diversified assets, tax planning, access planning, and flexibility after retirement begins."]},
{heading:"Coast FIRE and Barista FIRE use different targets",text:["Not every financial-independence strategy requires a fully funded portfolio today. Coast FIRE asks whether existing investments may grow to a traditional retirement target without further contributions, while Barista or semi-retirement approaches assume ongoing earned income covers part of current spending. These strategies require different cash-flow models from full FIRE. A calculator labeled FIRE number should make clear whether the target assumes zero employment income after the retirement date or whether continuing income is explicitly deducted from spending needs."]},
{heading:"Portfolio allocation affects sustainable withdrawals",text:["A withdrawal percentage cannot be evaluated independently of the assets supporting it. A portfolio concentrated in one stock, cryptocurrency, or cash has a different risk profile from a diversified mix of equities and high-quality bonds. Expected return alone is insufficient because volatility and sequence risk influence sustainability. FIRE users should test the target with an allocation they could realistically hold through a severe bear market. If the assumed return requires more risk than the retiree can tolerate, the spending target or retirement date may need adjustment."]},
{heading:"Flexibility has measurable value",text:["A household willing to reduce discretionary spending after poor market years has more options than one whose entire budget is fixed. Travel, gifts, upgrades, and other flexible categories can act as a buffer, while housing, insurance, food, and healthcare are harder to cut quickly. When interpreting the FIRE number, separate essential from discretionary annual spending. This does not justify underfunding essentials; it shows how much of the withdrawal plan can respond to market conditions instead of forcing identical inflation-adjusted withdrawals every year."]},
{heading:"Reaching FIRE and staying FIRE are different problems",text:["The accumulation calculation asks when assets may reach the target. The retirement calculation asks whether those assets can support withdrawals through uncertain markets, inflation, taxes, and longevity. A strong plan switches focus as the date approaches: reduce reliance on optimistic return assumptions, confirm health coverage and account access, establish cash reserves, and decide how spending will respond to a downturn. The FIRE number is therefore a checkpoint inside a broader retirement system, not a certificate that future work is permanently unnecessary."]},
]},
